<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm at School. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Places I Can Go</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm at School.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe routines and simple procedures. Interactive: Participate in simple dialogues on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe routines using location phrases (e.g., "I go to school in the morning."). Can participate in dialogue and answer questions about locations using simple terms (e.g., "Where are you?" "I'm at the market."). Can describe personal experiences by asking questions about familiar places (e.g., "Are you at school?" "I'm at school.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 17%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of target vocabulary and structures for speaking about locations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Where Am I?" game. The teacher shows flashcards of different places (e.g., school, park, market, library, gym, cafeteria) one by one. Students call out the place. The teacher then quickly points to a flashcard and asks, "Where am I?" Students respond with the place name. The teacher then introduces the full sentence "I'm at the [place]." (e.g., "I'm at the school.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target phrases: "I'm at school." and "Are you at the market?" using clear pronunciation. The teacher emphasizes the /sk/ sound in "school" and the /m/ sound in "market." The teacher uses flashcards to visually support the phrases, pointing to a place and saying, "I'm at the [place]." The teacher also models polite expressions like "Excuse me," and "Thank you."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple concept checking questions and examples:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a flashcard of a hospital: "Am I at school?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a flashcard of a school: "Am I at school?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the word "at": "Does 'at' mean 'in' or 'near' a place?" (Expected answer: "Yes, 'at' is for a place!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Places I Can Go" through short, illustrated dialogues for speaking practice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue script displayed on the board or projector, showing two children having a conversation at different locations. The dialogue incorporates target vocabulary (home, school, market, park, library, cafeteria) and grammar (I'm, at, Present simple, Where are you?).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue:</b><br>
                    Girl 1: "Hi! Where are you?"<br>
                    Girl 2: "I'm at the park. It's big!"<br>
                    Girl 1: "Oh, fun! I'm at school. I study here."<br>
                    Girl 2: "Are you at the library at school?"<br>
                    Girl 1: "No, I'm not. I'm in the cafeteria. It's busy!"<br>
                    Girl 2: "Your school is big! See you later!"<br>
                    Girl 1: "Thank you! Bye!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participate in simple dialogues on familiar topics, specifically asking and answering questions about locations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud. Students listen. Then, the teacher asks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is at the park?" (Expected answer: "Girl 2.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is Girl 1 at home?" (Expected answer: "No, she's at school.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where is Girl 1 at school?" (Expected answer: "In the cafeteria.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking: Controlled and semi-controlled practice using target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using contractions (I'm), prepositions of place (at, in, near), and the Present Simple tense to describe locations and answer questions. Focus on vocabulary: home, school, market, park, library, store, hospital, pharmacy, bakery, restaurant, cafeteria, gym, playground, nurse's office. Pronunciation of /sk/ and /m/ and blending phonemes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Choral Repetition & Gap-Fill):</b> The teacher leads the class in choral repetition of key phrases: "I'm at school." "I'm at the market." "Are you at the park?" The teacher then presents sentences with gaps on the board (e.g., "I'm ___ the school." "Are you ___ the library?") and students orally complete them chorally and individually, focusing on the correct preposition of place.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Substitution Drill with Flashcards):</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a place (e.g., "bakery"). The teacher models: "I'm at the bakery." Students repeat. Then, the teacher holds up a different flashcard (e.g., "hospital") and prompts students to say "I'm at the hospital." The teacher can also ask "Are you at the [place]?" and students respond "Yes, I'm at the [place]." or "No, I'm not. I'm at the [other place]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Question & Answer Chain):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of place flashcards. Student A picks a flashcard (without showing it) and asks Student B, "Where are you?" Student B guesses, "Are you at the [park]?" Student A responds "No, I'm not. I'm at the [library]." or "Yes, I'm at the [park]!" They swap roles. The teacher circulates, correcting pronunciation and grammar, especially /sk/ in "school" and /m/ in "market".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Places I Can Go" in a collaborative and fluent manner.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs using "phone calls."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Phone a Friend" Role-Play:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Each student receives a picture of a different location (e.g., Student A has "school," Student B has "market").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students pretend to call each other on the phone (using their hands as phones).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A starts: "Hello! Where are you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B answers: "Hi! I'm at the market. It's busy!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A responds: "Oh! I'm at school. I'm in the cafeteria. It's crowded!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B can then ask a question: "Are you at the gym at school?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A replies: "No, I'm not. I'm in the cafeteria. Your market sounds nice!" (incorporating polite expressions for compliments)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Thank you! See you later!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students switch roles and use new location flashcards. The teacher encourages use of adjectives (big, small, busy, quiet, clean, crowded) and polite expressions (excuse me, please, thank you).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Phone a Friend" role-play activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the pair role-play, asking and answering questions about locations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce words like "school" (/sk/) and "market" (/m/) clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use at least 3-4 location nouns (e.g., school, market, library) and prepositions (at, in).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of communication:</b> Students' questions and answers are understandable.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students actively participate in the dialogue with their partner.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of polite expressions:</b> Students attempt to use "Thank you" or "Excuse me."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can say 'I'm at the _______.' (Yes/No)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can ask 'Where are you?' (Yes/No)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My favorite place to talk about is: _______"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson's effectiveness and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new places did you talk about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about talking in English today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking-focused lesson? (e.g., student engagement with role-play, clear pronunciation of /sk/ and /m/ sounds, effective use of prepositions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., more practice with specific adjectives to describe places, reviewing question formation for "Are you...?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to confidently ask and answer questions about locations? Was the A1.1 level appropriate for the speaking tasks?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will build on their ability to describe and confirm locations by writing simple sentences and short paragraphs about where people are and what they do at those places, integrating the vocabulary and grammar practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>